<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elgin Marbles</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parthenon Marbles, Parthenon Sculptures</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Elgin Marbles are ancient Greek sculptures from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> and other buildings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a>, most made in the 5th century BCE under the direction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a>. Between 1801 and 1812 agents of Thomas Bruce, 7th Earl of Elgin, removed about half the surviving Parthenon sculptures from Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and shipped them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>; Elgin said he had the Ottoman officials' permission, and the legality is disputed. A parliamentary inquiry of 1816 found the acquisition legal and they passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Greece formally asked for their return in 1983, and the dispute continues, each side setting out its own case.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elgin-marbles.jpg" alt="Elgin Marbles" width="330" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jay.M · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elgin-marbles-jan-2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> was built on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a> from 447 BCE as a temple to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and it is likely that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a> was responsible for its sculptural design. Over later centuries it became a church and a mosque and its sculptures were badly damaged. On 26 September 1687, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morean-war">Sixth Ottoman–Venetian War</a>, a Venetian shell ignited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> stored there by the Turks. The explosion blew out the centre of the building and brought down about three fifths of the frieze.</p><p>Thomas Bruce, 7th Earl of Elgin, was appointed ambassador to the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a> in November 1798. The British government had no interest in sending artists to record the sculptures, so he employed artists himself under the Neapolitan painter Giovanni Lusieri. In 1801 he began to remove material, and he reported that fallen marble was being burned for lime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pieces Elgin acquired include about 21 figures from the east and west pediments, 15 of the original 92 metope panels showing battles between Lapiths and centaurs, and 75 metres of the frieze. They are more than half of what survives of the building's sculptural decoration. He also took a caryatid from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erechtheion">Erechtheion</a> and four slabs of the parapet frieze of the Temple of Athena Nike. To move them, capitals and many metopes and frieze slabs were hacked from the structure or sawn into sections, doing irreparable damage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>When Elgin displayed the first shipment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1807 it was an instant success with many. Richard Payne Knight declared the sculptures Roman additions or the work of inferior craftsmen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> denounced Elgin as a vandal in Childe Harold's Pilgrimage of 1812. A House of Commons committee in 1816 heard that the removal had been authorised by an Ottoman firman of July 1801, and the British Museum Act 1816 transferred the collection on condition that it be kept together and named the Elgin Marbles.</p><p>Supporters of return cite legal, moral, cultural and conservation arguments and the value of a unified display near the monument. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> argues that Elgin acquired them legally, that he rescued them from destruction, and that its collection lets them be seen alongside other ancient cultures. It says it is open to lending but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> will not accept the standard clause acknowledging its ownership.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elgin Marbles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since independence in 1960 Nigeria has sought the return of the bronzes, a case regarded as a test of the debate over restitution of cultural objects from Africa, comparable to the <b>Elgin Marbles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
